{
  "id": "crossing-the-line",
  "name": "Crossing the Line",
  "category": "routing",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "xp": 250,
  "level": 5,
  "description": "Two subnets, a router in the middle, and nothing gets through.",
  "brief": "Sales sits in 192.168.10.0/24 and the server room in 192.168.20.0/24. Router-01 already has an address on both networks and is cabled to both switches.\n\nThe machines still cannot reach across. A host only knows how to talk to its own subnet — for anything else it needs to be told which router to hand the packet to.\n\nGive every end device the right default gateway.",
  "hints": [
    "Run 'show ip route' on Router-01: it already knows both networks.",
    "A PC's default gateway must be a router address on the PC's own subnet.",
    "The gateway for the 192.168.10.0 side is 192.168.10.1.",
    "Do not forget Server-01 — it needs a gateway to send replies back."
  ],
